WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:04.620
In December 2016, a remote community in Siberia

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:07.840
experienced a mysterious outbreak.

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:13.180
90 people were hospitalized, and a 12-year-old boy died.

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:17.160
Soon, Russian officials identified what had killed him:

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:20.960
The deadly infectious disease anthrax.

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:24.200
The outbreak had started among reindeer.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:29.220
To contain the spread, they burned over 2,000 reindeer carcasses.

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:34.360
The strange thing was, there hadn't been an anthrax outbreak in the area

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:36.700
for more than 70 years.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:42.860
So, to figure out where it came from, scientists started looking underground.

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:51.780
In the coldest parts of the world, there's a layer of the Earth that stays frozen all year.

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:54.800
Every summer, the soil above it thaws,

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.140
but this deeper layer stays hard as rock.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:00.740
This is permafrost.

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:07.760
Most permafrost is here, in the Northern Hemisphere around the Arctic.

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:13.320
And because it never thaws,
permafrost acts kind of like the freezer in your kitchen.

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:17.140
When plants and animals
here die, they don't actually decompose.

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:22.360
Instead, they become preserved in the
frozen earth, like a time capsule.

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:26.180
And it's been that way for thousands of years.

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:27.880
But that's changing.

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:33.840
Today, humans are burning carbon and
making the atmosphere warmer.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:37.900
And that's causing the permafrost to thaw and shrink.

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:43.320
By 2100, only these areas will still have any permafrost.

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:46.040
And that's causing some problems.

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:51.860
When permafrost melts, the land above it becomes unstable, which can lead to landslides.

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:57.300
Man-made structures start to fall apart, as the ground underneath them collapses.

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:03.780
And dead plants and animals that had been frozen for years are starting to thaw out.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:12.120
As they're exposed to air and
bacteria, this organic material starts to decompose.

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:16.620
That releases greenhouse gases
like carbon dioxide and methane.

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:19.380
But that's not all it releases.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:24.000
In Siberia, scientists think that the anthrax outbreak

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:29.260
came from a long-dead reindeer
carcass that thawed out along with the permafrost.

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:32.520
Anthrax spores from the carcass would have spread across the area

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:35.360
and infected reindeer grazing nearby.

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:38.720
And it's not just anthrax.

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:43.320
Scientists worry that, as permafrost
melts, it could unearth all sorts of diseases

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:46.440
we thought we had under control.

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:52.180
35 million people live around permafrost.

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:54.640
But the carbon released, as permafrost melts,

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:57.860
will accelerate the impacts of
climate change everywhere:

00:02:57.860 --> 00:02:59.460
Rising seas,

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:00.860
heat waves,

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:04.520
droughts in some places, and
floods in others.

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:09.020
And now we can add one more thing to that list,

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:12.660
diseases we thought we conquered.

